The kiosk says what FCPM says: content here, renderer elsewhere

Station-node built a welcome screen for the studio and it was reading a
placeholder file it had invented on its own side. Autumn's call is that the
content belongs here — *"I can't tell them all like the specifics about our
SSIDs and stuff because it just simply belongs here. Like we're the ones who are
supposed to be generating this content."*

So this repository now produces the kiosk's content and stops there. Nothing
here renders anything, and the arrangement is deliberately temporary: station-node
is guest-hosting the screen while FCPM has no machine of its own, and the
artifact is shaped so that when that changes, **the artifact does not.**

## What this adds

| | |
|---|---|
| `kiosk/content.yml` | hand-written. The greeting, the room, the panel wording |
| `kiosk/welcome.yml` | **generated and committed.** The only file a kiosk reads |
| `bin/build-kiosk.py` | joins the first to `site/_data/` and writes the second |
| `bin/test_build_kiosk.py` | 26 tests, wired into `smoke.yml` alongside `--check` |
| `docs/KIOSK.md` | the whole arrangement and why each refusal is in it |

At the **root**, not in `site/`: a kiosk is not a web page, and `site/` is what
the public gets. The generator reads *down* into `site/_data/`, which is the
sanctioned direction — the first traffic that way, so `NODE.md` gains a note.

## Why generated and not hand-written

Because the alternative is typing the SSID twice. `site/_data/wifi.yml` owns the
network name along with the two things that make it trustworthy — a `confirmed:`
gate, and the record that this network has at least once simply not been there. A
second copy elsewhere has none of that attached, and a kiosk is the worst place
for that drift: a guest standing at the desk cannot tell "the screen is wrong"
from "the Wi-Fi is down", so they conclude the second one.

The generator inherits the gate. An unconfirmed SSID refuses to build, exactly as
`make-wifi-qr.py` refuses to print.

## Three refusals, each tested

- **No Wi-Fi password, and no QR encoding one.** This repository is public; a
  Wi-Fi QR is a font, not encryption. The check-in QR *is* carried, because it
  encodes a plain public URL. Guard runs against a hostile case — password in the
  sources and in the environment.
- **No hostname, port or address for itself.** This is what makes the eventual
  handover to an FCPM node free.
- **No timestamp.** It would churn every run and stop meaning anything.

## Being live

Her follow-up: *"I want it to be able to be live… at minimum that an update can
bounce it."* The renderer already live-reads per request, so a reload finds an
edit — but a screen on a wall has nobody to reload it.

So the artifact carries `revision:`, a twelve-character digest, first field in the
file. **It changes when the content changes and never otherwise**, which is the
half a timestamp does not have. A kiosk polls it and reloads on a difference.
`--check` in CI makes the promise hold: a forgotten regeneration fails a PR.

## One finding that is not about the kiosk

`docs/OPEN.md` gains an entry. `/check-in/` keeps name, reason, note **and
email** in `localStorage` and prefills them — correct on a phone, inverted on a
shared browser, where it shows the last visitor to the next one. The realistic
case is a staffer opening the page on the desk machine to help somebody. True
today, kiosk or no kiosk; recorded undecided.

## Deploy, for the record

Station-node asked whether it could publish this site from its own shelf. No: the
live publisher is Cloudflare's git build, and #80 already removed a second path
on the grounds that *"two ways to publish one site is a way to be confused about
which one did."* Answered and agreed with over there. **The kiosk needs no
deploy at all** — it is a committed file read off disk, which is why it is not
waiting on any of that.

## Still placeholder, and marked as such

The greeting, where the Wi-Fi card hangs, and who a guest should ask. Three
sentences somebody in the building should write. The greeting deliberately does
*not* reuse Bryan's mission statement — that is his verbatim wording for the
homepage, and borrowing it for a screen he has not seen is a paraphrase with
extra steps.
